[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its own table shows
two causes, and each got a ticket. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: `parser` fixed,
`hint` won't fix.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. The table below marks <code>hint</code> "no ticket of its own". It has one now.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The two problems

**`parser`: [ticket #1](https://github.com/frameiteasy/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).**
The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so
their dates come out with day and month swapped: booked 2 March, invited for 3 February. **Fixed**
(`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`); ticket closed. The reports the note under the table reads as a one-day move are
filed here by tenant and counted in this row.

**`hint`: [ticket #3](https://github.com/frameiteasy/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3).**
The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the hint says,
and the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The symptom looks the same as `parser`,
so intake cannot tell them apart, and the parser fix does not touch it. It happens only on the
`form` channel of booking-v3 tenants. **Won't fix**: the booking-v3 form is retired; ticket
closed.

## How they were counted

- With the desk's own view, as exported: the table above, one report per id, by tenant and
  channel. The counts on both tickets are its two rows.
- Not by intake's tag: intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and the table shows how often
  it disagrees.
- Not by subject: the same subject line covers both problems
  ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone; that is how
  both ended up in one ticket).

